Factitious Disorder
Mental Health & Substance Use
Falsification of symptoms/signs, Deception without external rewards, Eagerness for invasive procedures

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MHS-023
Reason for Visit: Recurrent, unexplained severe abdominal pain and localized skin abscesses, demanding urgent intravenous antibiotics and narcotic analgesia.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted severe, unremitting abdominal pain and the sudden appearance of multiple purulent skin lesions, with the patient vehemently insisting on immediate intravenous intervention. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to assess for a true acute abdomen or systemic sepsis, which could be life-threatening if delayed, while clinically evaluating the suspiciously complex presentation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My rare autoimmune condition is flaring again. I have a fever of 40 degrees, these lesions are necrotizing, and I need IV Dilaudid and Meropenem immediately or I will go into shock."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a highly complex, chaotic medical history. They report acute, 10/10 abdominal pain and rapidly spreading skin lesions. However, upon detailed questioning, there is a clear pattern of falsification of physical or psychological signs or symptoms. The patient demonstrates an extensive knowledge of medical terminology and hospital routines. There is evidence of intentional induction of injury or disease; the current skin lesions appear suspiciously geometric and localized only to dominant-hand-reachable areas. The patient presents themselves to others as profoundly ill, impaired, or injured, exhibiting deceptive behavior evident even in the absence of obvious external rewards (e.g., no pending lawsuits or disability claims). There is a marked eagerness to undergo invasive procedures or surgeries, alongside a history of numerous hospitalizations at different institutions under various specialists. Previous discharge summaries hint at the alteration of medical records or tampering with laboratory specimens, noting symptoms that inconsistently worsen when discharge is planned.
Functional Status: Claims to be completely bedbound and agonizingly ill, yet mobilized easily to unlock the front door.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of iatrogenic harm from unnecessary medical procedures; severe underlying psychiatric pathology.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/80 | HR: 78 | Temp: 37.1 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Appears comfortable when unobserved, but adopts a pained expression and writhing movements when directly examined. No signs of shock.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular and respiratory systems are entirely normal. Abdominal exam reveals diffuse tenderness to very light palpation, but entirely negative for guarding, rigidity, or rebound. Bowel sounds are normal. Integumentary exam reveals three distinct, circular, superficially excoriated lesions on the left anterior thigh, surrounded by mild erythema. They do not appear necrotizing or deeply purulent; they strongly resemble self-inflicted chemical or thermal burns. The patient's claimed fever is absent on objective measurement. Findings support falsification of symptoms/signs, deception without external rewards, and an eagerness for invasive procedures.
Environmental Observations: A large, highly organized binder of medical records and printouts from medical journals is prominently displayed on the bedside table. Empty syringes and unidentifiable topical solutions visible in the bathroom wastebin.
Medication Reconciliation: Claims allergies to all non-narcotic analgesics and basic oral antibiotics.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Factitious Disorder - Status: Exacerbated (Active symptom production)
Differentials:

Somatic Symptom Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely because the patient is intentionally producing/faking the symptoms and lesions, whereas in somatic symptom disorder, the physical symptoms are genuinely experienced without conscious deception.

Malingering - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of clear external incentives (e.g., financial gain, avoiding legal prosecution); the motivation appears to be entirely to assume the "sick role."

True Cutaneous Abscess/Sepsis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of objective fever, normal hemodynamics, and the highly atypical, geometric presentation of the lesions.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Risk of self-harm and iatrogenic injury)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with claims of severe, life-threatening symptoms demanding immediate, high-risk interventions (IV narcotics/broad-spectrum antibiotics). Telephone triage was forced to treat the reported symptoms as a potential emergency. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was absolutely crucial to objectively evaluate the clinical signs. The examination revealed a stark discrepancy between the subjective claims (10/10 pain, high fever, necrotizing lesions) and the objective reality (normal vitals, soft abdomen, superficial self-inflicted wounds). Denying the patient's demands without a physical examination would risk missing a genuine emergency, while assessing them in person allowed for the safe, definitive refusal of unnecessary, dangerous iatrogenic interventions, managing the risk appropriately in the home.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Firmly and professionally declined the administration of intravenous narcotics or antibiotics, explaining that the objective clinical findings do not support systemic infection or an acute surgical abdomen. Applied simple dry dressings to the superficial skin lesions. Expected investigation results: toxicology screens or forensic review revealing deliberate induction.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement "Scheduled Checking." Advise the patient that medical attention will be provided on a strictly scheduled basis (e.g., a weekly 15-minute GP appointment), regardless of symptom severity. Explain that this prevents unnecessary emergency visits. This operant conditioning removes the acute attention/reward of emergency presentations, reinforcing that the "sick role" will only be validated within controlled, non-escalating boundaries.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Nil.
DME/Safety (Optional): Nil.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1 week.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or call 000 only if objective, measurable signs of severe illness occur, such as a witnessed loss of consciousness, inability to walk, or profuse bleeding.

